    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve this task, we need to iterate through the powers of 11 starting from 11<sup>1</sup>  and check whether each power of 11 is a palindrome or not. If not, we will take it as the smallest integer power of 11 that is not a palindrome.\nLet's start by computing the powers of 11 starting from 11<sup>1</sup>.\n11<sup>1</sup> = 11\n11<sup>2</sup> = 121\n11<sup>3</sup> = 1331\n11<sup>4</sup> = 14641\n11<sup>5</sup> = 1594321\n11<sup>6</sup> = 1771561\n11<sup>7</sup> = 19487171\n11<sup>8</sup> = 214358881\n11<sup>9</sup> = 2357947691\nWe can observe that 11<sup>2</sup>, 11<sup>3</sup>, 11<sup>6</sup>, 11<sup>7</sup>, 11<sup>8</sup>, and 11<sup>9</sup> all are palindromes. However, 11<sup>4</sup> = 14641 is not a palindrome. Thus, the smallest positive integer that is both an integer power of 11 and is not a palindrome is 11<sup>4</sup>  = 14641. So, we can conclude  that the answer is 14641.\n\nAnswer:\nYes",
